{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 2, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a member of the House of Tripoli and a Crusader. He was born in 1152 AD to Amalric I of Jerusalem and his wife Maria Comnena. His father Amalric was the King of Jerusalem and his mother Maria was the niece of the Byzantine Emperor Manuel I Comnenus. Raymond III was the younger brother of Baldwin IV of Jerusalem and Sibylla of Jerusalem.\n\n* **Family and Early Life*** \nRaymond III was born in 1152 AD and he was the second son of Amalric I of Jerusalem and his wife Maria Comnena. His father Amalric was the King of Jerusalem and his mother Maria was the niece of the Byzantine Emperor Manuel I Comnenus. Raymond III was the younger brother of Baldwin IV of Jerusalem and Sibylla of Jerusalem.\n\nRaymond III married Eschiva of Bures in 1165 AD. They had one son named Bohemond IV of Antioch and Tripoli. After the death of Eschiva of Bures in 1184 AD Raymond III married Alix of Jerusalem in 1183 AD.\n\n* **Count of Tripoli*** \nRaymond III became the Count of Tripoli in 1152 AD after the death of his father Amalric I of Jerusalem. He ruled the county for nearly 40 years and he was known for his military campaigns against the Muslim forces in the region. Raymond III was also known for his diplomatic skills and he maintained good relations with the Byzantine Empire and the Kingdom of Jerusalem.\n\nIn 1187 AD Raymond III joined the forces of the King of Jerusalem Guy of Lusignan in the Battle of Hattin. The battle was a disaster for the Crusaders and Raymond III was taken prisoner by the Muslim forces. He was later released in 1188 AD in exchange for a ransom.\n\n* **Later Life and Death*** \nAfter his release from captivity Raymond III returned to the County of Tripoli and he continued to rule the county until his death in 1187 AD. He died in 1187 AD and he was succeeded by his son Bohemond IV of Antioch and Tripoli.", "logprobs